THREAD - Computah OS library
(generated from docs/API.md)

Threads for programs: functions that run alongside
the current program. A thread is attached to the
process that created it and is killed when that
process ends, unless detached.

thread.create(fn, ...) -> thread

Starts fn(...) in a new thread and returns a
thread object:

Method - Description
t:status() - "running", "ready", "waiting",
    "suspended" or "dead"
t:join([timeout]) - wait for it to end; true, or
    false on timeout
t:kill(), t:suspend(), t:resume() - stop / pause /
    continue
t:detach(), t:attach() - keep running in the
    background / stop with the parent
t.id - its process id (see ps)

  local t = thread.create(function(n)
    for i = 1, n do print("tick " .. i) os.sleep(1) end
  end, 3)
  t:join()

thread.current() -> thread

The running thread record, or nil.

thread.waitForAll(threads[, timeout]) -> boolean

Waits until every thread in the list ends. False
if the timeout passed first.

thread.waitForAny(threads[, timeout]) -> boolean

Waits until any thread in the list ends. False if
the timeout passed first.
